<aside class="examples" aria-labelledby="ex-h">
<h2 class="sec" id="ex-h">Worked examples</h2>
<section class="excard" id="ex-a" aria-labelledby="ex-a-h">
<h3 id="ex-a-h">Example A · Path A, from the trail</h3>
<dl>
<dt>Where I found it:</dt>
<dd>The CST286 trail How fast will you spin?, stops 3 and 4.</dd>
<dt>My relationship:</dt>
<dd>When nothing outside twists a spinning system, moment of inertia times spin rate stays the same, so the new spin rate is the old one times the old moment of inertia divided by the new one: <code>&omega;&#8322; = &omega;&#8321; &times; I&#8321; &divide; I&#8322;</code>, where <code>&omega;</code> is the spin rate in radians per second (rad/s) and <code>I</code> is the moment of inertia in kilogram square meters (kg&middot;m&sup2;).</dd>
<dt>My condition:</dt>
<dd>Nothing outside the spinning system twists it while the mass moves in or out.</dd>
<dt>My check · Before:</dt>
<dd>On the chair, with two 1.5 kg bottles at 0.75 m and then 0.20 m, a body estimate of 2.0 kg&middot;m&sup2;, and 3.0 rad/s arms out, I predict 5.2 rad/s arms in.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I did:</dt>
<dd>Recorded my spin with phyphox, phone flat against my chest, two turns arms out and two turns arms in.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I got:</dt>
<dd>5.0 rad/s.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I make of it:</dt>
<dd>about 4% below my prediction. The chair's bearing twists against the spin a little, which breaks my condition slightly, and my body estimate was rough; solving for my own body's moment of inertia gives 2.23 kg&middot;m&sup2;.</dd>
</dl>
</section>
<section class="excard" id="ex-b" aria-labelledby="ex-b-h">
<h3 id="ex-b-h">Example B · Path B, the playground swing</h3>
<dl>
<dt>Where I found it:</dt>
<dd>OpenStax College Physics, section 16.4, The Simple Pendulum.</dd>
<dt>My relationship:</dt>
<dd>The time for one full swing grows with the square root of the length: <code>T = 2&pi; &radic;(L &divide; g)</code>, where <code>T</code> is the period in seconds (s), <code>L</code> the length in meters (m) and <code>g</code> = 9.8 m/s&sup2;.</dd>
<dt>My condition:</dt>
<dd>The swing moves through a small angle, less than about 15 degrees from straight down, and air drag is small.</dd>
<dt>My check · Before:</dt>
<dd>A phone on a 1.0 m string should take 2.0 s per swing.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I did:</dt>
<dd>Timed ten swings with a stopwatch, starting about 10 degrees out.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I got:</dt>
<dd>20.3 s for ten swings, so 2.03 s per swing.</dd>
<dt>My check · What I make of it:</dt>
<dd>about 1% above the prediction. The length to the middle of the phone was hard to measure, and about 2 cm more length would account for the difference.</dd>
</dl>
</section>
